>   raised at: stdIn:1.8-1.18
val h2: int -> nat = fn i => i;
> stdIn:1.9-1.19 Error: cannot claim a checked function type 'int -> nat'
>   raised at: stdIn:1.9-1.19
val h3: nat -> int = fn i => i;
> stdIn:1.9-1.19 Error: cannot claim a checked function type 'nat -> int'
>   raised at: stdIn:1.9-1.19

(*) A function type nested inside another type is rejected too.
val h4: {f: nat -> nat} = {f = fn i => i};
> stdIn:1.9-1.24 Error: cannot claim a checked function type '{f:nat -> nat}'
>   raised at: stdIn:1.9-1.24

(*) So is one claimed at a parameter, or by an ascription or a conversion.
fun apply2 (g: nat -> nat) = g 1;
> stdIn:1.16-1.26 Error: cannot claim a checked function type 'nat -> nat'
>   raised at: stdIn:1.16-1.26
val h5 = (fn i => i) : nat -> nat;
> stdIn:1.24-1.34 Error: cannot claim a checked function type 'nat -> nat'
>   raised at: stdIn:1.24-1.34

(*) A function type that constrains nothing is unaffected.
val h6: int -> int = fn i => i;
> val h6 = fn : int -> int
val h7: plain -> plain = fn i => i;
> val h7 = fn : plain -> plain

(* A binding whose annotation has a different erasure is an ordinary type
 * error, as the conversion above was; the unifier names both, and says which
 * is an alias. *)
val bad2: nat = "abc";
